  "problem": "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2y\\ge {x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\u2202A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?",
  "worked_solution": "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $\u2202A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $\u2202A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/0fc95ee69ce7121c0cf8ae68029b135d9667f50275227d141f1d979f85408859.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the  parabola $-2y={x}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\u2202A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2y={x}^{2}\\,}.\n$$",
